## Next.js App Router (next-intl SSR)

- **File-based routing** - files in `app/` directory automatically become routes
- **Document routes live under `app/[locale]/*`** - next-intl SSR with `localePrefix: 'always'` (`i18n/routing.ts`); the next-intl middleware composed in `proxy.ts` redirects/rewrites unprefixed document requests to the locale-prefixed URL
- **Server Components by default** - all pages are Server Components unless marked with 'use client'
- **Non-document surfaces stay OUTSIDE `[locale]`**: `app/api/**` route handlers, `app/actions/<action>.ts` Server Actions, `app/sitemap.ts`, `app/robots.ts`, `app/dev/ui` (404 in production via `proxy.ts`)

## Route Structure (real map)

```
app/                            # no app/layout.tsx: the layouts below are the root layouts
  ├── global-error.tsx          # Root catastrophic error UI (own <html>, fixed lang="en")
  ├── providers.tsx             # Client providers (analytics init on load/idle)
  ├── [locale]/
  │   ├── layout.tsx            # ROOT layout: <html lang>, fonts, WebVitalsReporter, Providers,
  │   │                         #   title.default + title.template, requireLocale → getMessages →
  │   │                         #   NextIntlClientProvider wraps Header / main / Footer
  │   ├── page.tsx              # Route: /en (home)
  │   ├── error.tsx             # Locale-scoped error boundary
  │   ├── not-found.tsx         # Locale-scoped 404
  │   ├── [...rest]/page.tsx    # Catch-all: notFound(), so an unmatched path gets that 404 in the layout
  │   └── example-form/
  │       └── page.tsx          # Route: /en/example-form
  ├── api/                      # Route handlers: health, vitals, csp-report, example-form
  ├── actions/                  # Server Actions (by domain)
  ├── dev/ui/                   # Dev playground, the second root layout (production returns 404)
  ├── sitemap.ts                # Dynamic sitemap with per-route hreflang entries
  └── robots.ts                 # robots.txt
```

No `loading.tsx` exists in the baseline — add one per segment only when a route
gains slow data fetching.

### Special Files

- **`layout.tsx`** - Shared layout for route segment and children
- **`error.tsx`** - Error UI (must be a Client Component); lives at `app/[locale]/error.tsx`
- **`global-error.tsx`** - Last-resort root error UI (`app/global-error.tsx`)
- **`not-found.tsx`** - 404 page (`app/[locale]/not-found.tsx`)
- **`route.ts`** - API route handler (`app/api/**`)

## Adding a Localized Route (danger zone)

Every `[locale]` page/layout entry MUST narrow the route param via
`requireLocale()`. The locale reaches `i18n/request.ts` through
`next/root-params` (the `[locale]` segment sits above the root layout), so no
entry pins it and there is no per-entry call to forget (SKELETONS "i18n").
Mirror `app/[locale]/page.tsx`:

```typescript
// app/[locale]/blog/[id]/page.tsx
import type { ReactElement } from 'react';

import { requireLocale } from '@/i18n/request-locale';

interface Props {
    params: Promise<{ locale: string; id: string }>;
}

const BlogPost = async ({ params }: Props): Promise<ReactElement> => {
    const { locale: rawLocale, id } = await params;
    const locale = requireLocale(rawLocale); // notFound() on unknown locale
    // ...
};

export default BlogPost;
```

- **A Server Action, a Route Handler and a metadata image cannot read root params** (Next throws there):
  pass the locale, `getTranslations({ locale, namespace })`. A Server Action gets it from its caller (the
  example form sends a `locale` field) and validates it with `hasLocale` before use, because it is client input
- **`i18n/request.ts` is the one reader of `locale` from `next/root-params`** (Server Components only); it
  validates the value against `routing.locales` and falls back to the default. Server Components get the
  locale through next-intl (`useTranslations`, `getTranslations`), not by calling the getter again
- **Dynamic segments** work as usual, nested under the locale:
  `app/[locale]/<segment>/[id]/page.tsx` → `/en/<segment>/:id`; catch-all `[...slug]`,
  optional catch-all `[[...slug]]`
- **Params are async** in Next 16: `params: Promise<{ locale: string; ... }>` —
  always `await` them

## Metadata and SEO

- **Use `generateMetadata`** with `buildPageMetadata` (`shared/lib/pageMetadata.ts`): it reads `meta.<page>` from the messages and returns the title, description, canonical, `alternates.languages`, `openGraph` and `twitter`
- **Every localized route builds `alternates.languages`** from `routing.locales`; `app/sitemap.ts` mirrors them as `<xhtml:link>` hreflang entries
- **A page states its own `openGraph` and `twitter` in full.** Next merges metadata one key deep, so a page's `openGraph` replaces the layout's whole object: a page that sets only a `title` shares the home page's `og:url` and loses its image. The helper does this for you; keep using it for every new page
- **A page that uses the helper has an `opengraph-image.tsx` beside its `page.tsx`.** The image file is attached to the segment that holds it, and the layout's does not reach a page whose `openGraph` replaced the layout's. A guard test (`shared/lib/pageMetadata.test.ts`) fails when it is missing; the one-line shape is a re-export of the site card in `app/[locale]/opengraph-image.tsx`

```typescript
// app/[locale]/page.tsx
export const generateMetadata = async ({ params }: HomePageProps): Promise<Metadata> => {
    const { locale } = await params;
    return buildPageMetadata({ locale: requireLocale(locale), page: 'home', path: '' });
};
```

```typescript
// app/[locale]/example-form/opengraph-image.tsx — reuses the site card; write your own to differ
export { default, size, contentType } from '@/app/[locale]/opengraph-image';
```

### title.template cascade (do not move)

`title.template` does **not** apply to the segment that defines it — only to
the segments below it. `app/[locale]/layout.tsx` owns `title.default` +
`title.template` (read from `meta.root` in the messages), and the page of that
same segment, the home page, would lose the brand suffix. So `buildPageMetadata`
returns `title: { absolute }` for the page at the locale root (`path: ''`) and a
bare title for every page below it. A new page at the locale root states its
whole title the same way; a page below it stays bare and the template wraps it.

## Layouts: two root layouts

There is no layout file directly under `app/`: a layout with no layout above it is a root layout,
and `next/root-params` exports the dynamic segments above the root layout (here
`locale`). A new top-level surface that renders HTML outside `[locale]` needs a
root layout of its own; moving between root layouts is a full page load.

| Layout                      | Responsibilities                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |
| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `app/[locale]/layout.tsx`   | Root layout: `<html lang>`, `<body>`, fonts, `globals.css`, `WebVitalsReporter`, `Providers`, `generateStaticParams` from `routing.locales`, `requireLocale`, `getMessages` → `NextIntlClientProvider` wrapping `Header` / `main` / `Footer`; `generateMetadata` (`metadataBase`, title, SEO). |
| `app/dev/ui/layout.tsx`     | Second root layout for the dev playground: `<html lang="en">`, `<body>`, the font and the dark theme; 404 in production.                                                                                                                                                             |

The layout chrome (Header/Footer) MUST stay inside `NextIntlClientProvider` —
it consumes translations and breaks (hydration mismatch / prerender failure)
when rendered outside the provider boundary.

## Navigation (typed, locale-aware)

- **NEVER import `Link` from `next/link`, or `useRouter` / `redirect` / `usePathname` from `next/navigation`** for document routes — they bypass the locale prefix and produce un-prefixed (broken) URLs
- **Always import from `@/i18n/navigation`** (`createNavigation(routing)`): `Link`, `redirect`, `usePathname`, `useRouter`, `getPathname`
- **Exception**: `notFound` stays in `next/navigation` (no localized variant)

```typescript
// Client Component
'use client';

import type { ReactElement } from 'react';

import { Link, useRouter } from '@/i18n/navigation';

export const Navigation = (): ReactElement => {
    const router = useRouter();

    return (
        <nav>
            {/* renders /en/example-form */}
            <Link href="/example-form">Example form</Link>
            <button
                onClick={() => {
                    router.push('/');
                }}
            >
                Home
            </button>
        </nav>
    );
};
```

## Route Organization Best Practices

- **New document route = new folder under `app/[locale]/`** — never at `app/` root
- **Route groups** `(group)/` still work under `[locale]` for organization without affecting URLs
- **Keep pages thin** - the RSC entry does locale narrowing + metadata; interactivity lives in a `*PageClient.tsx` client island beside the page
- **Locale set stays `['en']`** until the caller explicitly asks to expand it (`i18n/routing.ts` + `messages/<locale>.json`)
- **Lazy load** heavy client components with `next/dynamic`
